'''git commit saves your staged changes locally on your computer. It creates a snapshot of your project in your local repository's history, requiring a commit message to record what changed. No network or internet connection is involved.
 whereas git push uploads your local commits to a remote repository (like GitHub or GitLab) so others can see your work or so it is safely backed up off your local machine.'''
'''The -u flag is short for --set-upstream.
When you run git push -u origin main, Git links your local main branch directly to the main branch on the origin remote.'''
